my name is Emil Bruno I'm a postdoctoral  researcher in Rebecca Sachs's lab in  that brain and cognitive sciences  department at MIT the reason that I'm in  this game is that I was a volunteer at a  conflict resolution program and after  the program I saw that for a subset of  the kids it was really successful this  was in Ireland during the Troubles so  the kids in Belfast from the Catholic  neighborhoods would walk to the  Protestant neighborhoods to meet their  new friends which is actually quite a  dangerous activity back then but for  other kids for example for the half of  the kids at the camp a fight broke out  between two of them and it immediately  split the group down partisan lines and  there was a full-scale 150 child brawl  so obviously for that subset of the kids  the camp wasn't particularly successful  so the question I left with was well  what do we know about these programs  which programs work and which don't  why did some kids respond positively and  other kids not and when I came back and  started asking these questions I  realised there was very little there  very little was known and so that's why  I'm here the research program I'm  engaged in Rebecca Saxe is lab was  motivated by my experience with conflict  resolution programs so my goal is to  provide support for these conflict  resolution programs as they try to  understand what effect they're actually  having so there are a number of ways  that we're trying to address this one is  conflict resolution programs have a  number of different components in them  so we want to be able to assess these  little components and we've done this by  trying to establish controlled  experimental paradigms in the field in  regions where conflict exists for  example we've done that in the Middle  East we organised surprise online  conversations between Israelis and  Palestinians because we wanted to see  what aspect of these dialogue programs  that are commonly used what effect of  those have what we found for example is  that the type of intervention that works  best for Israelis does not work for  Palestinians and the intervention that  we're sure Palestinians does not work  for Israelis so this is important  because conflict resolution program  are based on the intuition that the same  intervention should be used across the  board for both participants so this is  one of the benefits I think that  evidence-based approaches can give they  can actually test these assumptions to  see whether they're true or not another  set of measures we're using are  neuroimaging measures and the goal of  that is to find neural markers of these  psychological biases that once we have a  neural marker for it we can then see if  the program affects that neural process  over the course of the program so one of  the things that we've been focused on is  the role of narratives in intergroup  conflicts if you ask Israelis and  Palestinians to explain why a barrier  was built by Israel around the West Bank  they have very different narratives  about that event they respond very  strongly to each other's narrative they  think that their narrative is reasonable  and rational and true and that the other  side's narrative is not only untrue but  completely irrational to believe so what  you can have people do to look at this  bias is you can have them read about  their own narratives and about the other  side's narratives and then you see how  their brain is responding to these  narratives and what we found is that  there is a good neural marker of the  response to these narratives the amount  of response in this brain region tracks  with how strongly they feel about the  other group another line of research is  on empathy one of the things that  characterizes intergroup conflicts is  that there's a big empathy gap between  an group and out-group and this gap  seems to be particularly important so  not just the empathy for the out-group  but not just the empathy for the  in-group of the the difference between  the two the way I'm looking at the nura  mission is twofold  so one it just is inherently interesting  to me to see these neural processes to  see if they're localized to see where  they are and what they interact with in  the brain so that that's just the  interesting component the potentially  important component is if we can get a  neural signature of these psychological  processes then we have a measure that we  can't get otherwise and that allows us  then to assess the effect of  intervention  that are aimed at decreasing that bias
 

